WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.325
Lesson 8.7.

00:00:02.325 --> 00:00:07.855
Quantitative risk: volatility, VaR, drawdown, correlation and sizing.

00:00:07.855 --> 00:00:09.779
By the end of this lesson, you'll

00:00:09.779 --> 00:00:11.816
be able to put numbers on portfolio

00:00:11.816 --> 00:00:13.740
risk and size positions from them.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.330
Here's the plan.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:18.015
First, the key ideas.

00:00:18.015 --> 00:00:20.730
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:22.255
Then, your checklist.

00:00:22.255 --> 00:00:24.417
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:25.717 --> 00:00:30.697
First, Volatility (σ): annualised standard deviation of returns.

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:33.643
Daily σ ≈ annual σ ÷

00:00:33.643 --> 00:00:38.292
√365 (crypto trades every day).

00:00:38.292 --> 00:00:41.525
Next, Value at risk (VaR): a loss

00:00:41.525 --> 00:00:45.424
level you'd expect to exceed only rarely

00:00:45.424 --> 00:00:48.197
(e.g. 1 day in 20 at 95%).

00:00:48.197 --> 00:00:51.843
Parametric 1-day VaR ≈ 1.65

00:00:51.843 --> 00:00:54.877
× daily σ × position.

00:00:54.877 --> 00:00:58.672
It assumes normal returns; crypto has fat tails,

00:00:58.672 --> 00:01:01.837
so real losses exceed VaR more often.

00:01:01.837 --> 00:01:05.677
Use it as a floor, plus stress tests (11.4).

00:01:05.677 --> 00:01:09.122
Then, Max drawdown: the largest peak-to-trough fall.

00:01:09.122 --> 00:01:10.739
Ask: could I live through it,

00:01:10.739 --> 00:01:12.937
and would my policies survive it?

00:01:12.937 --> 00:01:16.824
Also, Correlation: in crashes, correlations between

00:01:16.824 --> 00:01:19.292
crypto assets rise towards 1.

00:01:19.292 --> 00:01:21.593
Diversifying within crypto helps less

00:01:21.593 --> 00:01:23.225
when you need it most.

00:01:23.225 --> 00:01:27.054
And finally, Sizing: volatility targeting (size so each

00:01:27.054 --> 00:01:31.293
position contributes similar risk); the Kelly criterion gives

00:01:31.293 --> 00:01:34.370
a theoretical maximum bet size from edge and

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:37.925
odds, but estimates are noisy, so professionals use

00:01:37.925 --> 00:01:40.250
a small fraction of it or skip it.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.065
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:44.065 --> 00:01:50.059
$100,000 of ETH at 70% annual volatility:

00:01:50.059 --> 00:01:55.482
the calculator gives daily σ ≈ 3.66%,

00:01:55.482 --> 00:01:59.064
1-day 95% VaR ≈ $6,046.

00:01:59.064 --> 00:02:03.475
Real single-day falls have been far larger (in March

00:02:03.475 --> 00:02:07.104
2020, ETH fell by roughly 40% in a day).

00:02:07.104 --> 00:02:11.329
So: VaR for day-to-day sizing, stress tests for survival.

00:02:12.629 --> 00:02:14.089
Here's your checklist.

00:02:14.089 --> 00:02:15.624
Do it now, for real.

00:02:15.624 --> 00:02:20.599
First, Volatility and VaR computed for my largest positions.

00:02:20.599 --> 00:02:23.496
Next, Portfolio survives the worst historical

00:02:23.496 --> 00:02:25.434
drawdown of what it holds.

00:02:25.434 --> 00:02:28.492
Then, Correlation assumed to rise in stress.

00:02:28.492 --> 00:02:32.772
And finally, Sizing rule written (e.g. volatility targeting).

00:02:34.072 --> 00:02:35.282
Question 1.

00:02:35.282 --> 00:02:37.617
Annual volatility 50%.

00:02:37.617 --> 00:02:38.532
Daily σ?

00:02:42.952 --> 00:02:49.587
The answer: About 2.6% (50 ÷ √365).

00:02:50.887 --> 00:02:52.132
Question 2.

00:02:52.132 --> 00:02:54.577
Why is VaR a floor, not a ceiling?

00:02:58.997 --> 00:03:02.176
The answer: It assumes normal returns; crypto's

00:03:02.176 --> 00:03:04.692
fat tails make big losses more common.

00:03:05.992 --> 00:03:07.262
Question 3.

00:03:07.262 --> 00:03:09.457
What happens to correlations in a crash?

00:03:13.877 --> 00:03:16.342
The answer: They tend to rise towards 1.

00:03:17.642 --> 00:03:18.887
Let's recap.

00:03:18.887 --> 00:03:23.532
Volatility (σ): annualised standard deviation of returns.

00:03:23.532 --> 00:03:26.715
Value at risk (VaR): a loss level

00:03:26.715 --> 00:03:30.554
you'd expect to exceed only rarely (e.g.

00:03:30.554 --> 00:03:32.727
1 day in 20 at 95%).

00:03:32.727 --> 00:03:35.792
Max drawdown: the largest peak-to-trough fall.

00:03:35.792 --> 00:03:39.079
Correlation: in crashes, correlations between

00:03:39.079 --> 00:03:41.152
crypto assets rise towards 1.

00:03:42.452 --> 00:03:44.967
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:44.967 --> 00:03:48.212
Next up, Lesson 9.0: Mastery Starter.
